lving and responsibility but hot temper. A has technical but show-off and not open.\n\nProbably none perfect. Need to weigh. The most critical for long-distance pilot: reliability, stress, decision, crew cooperation. B's nasty remarks could be a safety risk. C's communication difficulty also risk. D's hot temper also risk. A's not open to new ideas may affect adaptability.\n\nWhich candidate has least severe concerns? Let's compare severity: B's nasty remarks = poor crew cooperation, could affect safety. C's difficulty communicating ideas = may affect crew coordination. D's hot temper = could affect crew environment. A's show-off and not open to new ideas = may affect teamwork but less direct.\n\nBut A lacks evidence on stress resilience and reliability. B has strong reliability. C has calm and decision. D has responsibility.\n\nMaybe B is strongest overall despite communication issue, but we need to consider seriousness. Nasty remarks could be severe. However, we have no evidence of actual behavior under pressure.\n\nC's egocentric and communication difficulty also severe.\n\nD's hot temper also severe.\n\nThus B might still be best due to high reliability and stress handling, and we could mitigate communication via training.\n\nThus I will recommend Candidate B.\n\nWe need to reference existing discussion (none) but can say \"Given we have not yet discussed, I propose...\"\n\nPublic message: state recommendation, give evidence: B very conscientious, handles stress well, good weather assessment, but note concern about nasty remarks, but weigh against strengths.\n\nOk."}
